.\" Man page for tngbackup
.\" Contact: Massimo "RedFoxy Darrest" Cicciò
.TH TNGBACKUP 1 "September 2026" "TNGBackup 2.0.1" "User Commands"
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH NAME
tngbackup \- The Next Generation Borg Backup management utility
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tngbackup
.RI [ OPERATION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B tngbackup
.BR backup " [" \-c
.IR FILE | DIR ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR URI ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PASSPHRASE ]
.RB [ \-\-archive
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-path
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-audit\-log
.IR FILE ]
.PP
.B tngbackup
.B \-h
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tngbackup
is a Bash wrapper around
.BR borg (1)
that centralises repository configuration, credential handling, retention
policy and audit logging. A complete backup routine is expressed in a single
configuration file and driven by one command, suitable for unattended
execution from
.BR cron (8)
or a
.BR systemd.timer (5).
.PP
All ten operations are implemented and are dispatched through a single shared
handler used identically by single-config and batch runs. The suite is covered
by
.I tests/dispatch\-test.sh
(26 checks, runs anywhere using a stub
.BR borg )
and
.I tests/integration\-test.sh
(exercises every operation against a throwaway repository; requires a real
Borg installation). See
.B TESTS
below.
.PP
Configuration is read from a file that is
.B sourced
as Bash, from environment variables, and from command line options. Because a
configuration file is executed, it must be owned by the invoking user and not
writable by others.
.PP
.B setup_borg_env
prepares the environment before every Borg invocation: it exports
.B BORG_PASSPHRASE
and
.BR BORG_REPO ,
and, when
.B REPO_URI
begins with
.BR ssh:// ,
also exports
.BI BORG_RSH= "ssh $SSH_OPT \-p $SSH_PORT"
so that
.B SSH_OPT
and
.B SSH_PORT
take effect automatically. For a local repository
.B BORG_RSH
is unset instead.
.PP
Borg is always invoked with standard input closed, and the script exports
.B BORG_UNKNOWN_UNENCRYPTED_REPO_ACCESS_IS_OK=yes
and
.BR BORG_RELOCATED_REPO_ACCESS_IS_OK=yes ,
so a confirmation prompt can never block an unattended run. Both variables
honour an existing value from the environment.
.PP
A trap on
.BR EXIT ,
.B INT
and
.B TERM
unsets the credential variables, destroys any temporary configuration file and
unmounts a leftover FUSE mount point. A successful
.B mount
clears the internal mount path first, so the trap never tears down a mount the
user asked for.
.PP
If no operation is given on the command line, an interactive menu is displayed
and read from standard input. The menu runs before the configuration is loaded
and only sets the operation; every other option given on the command line
remains in effect. Never invoke
.B tngbackup
without an operation from a non-interactive context, or it will block
indefinitely.
.PP
.B tngbackup
exits with the exit code of the operation it performed, following Borg's own
three-way convention: exit
.B 0
is recorded
.BR SUCCESS ,
exit
.B 1
is recorded
.B WARNING
and still exits
.BR 0 ,
and exit
.B 2
or higher is recorded
.B FAILED
and exits with that code. A warning means the operation completed and produced
its result - a backup that could not read a few files still created a usable
archive - so it is deliberately not treated as a failure. See
.B "EXIT STATUS"
below.
.SS Configuration precedence
The order of operations is: parse the command line, show the menu if needed,
save the repository and passphrase given on the command line, source the
configuration file, then re-apply those two saved values.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-p
.B Command line wins.
.B REPO_URI
and
.B REPO_PASSPHRASE
are re-applied after the configuration file has been sourced.
.TP
.BR \-\-archive ", " \-\-path ", " \-\-mountpoint ", " \-l ", " \-\-audit\-log
Applied
.B before
the configuration file is sourced, so a configuration file that assigns the
corresponding variable unconditionally overrides the command line. Omit the
assignment, or guard it as
.BR "VAR=\(dq${VAR:\-default}\(dq" ,
to let the command line win.
.TP
.B \-c
Always the command line value; it selects the file to source.
.PP
In batch mode
.B \-r
and
.B \-p
have no effect: the batch loop clears both before sourcing each configuration
file.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH OPERATIONS
The operation is the first non-option argument. Options may appear before or
after it. Because
.B BORG_REPO
is exported, the repository is usually addressed implicitly and archives with
the
.BI :: name
shorthand.
.TP
.B init
Create a new Borg repository at
.B REPO_URI
using
.BR BORG_ENCRYPTION .
Runs
.BR "borg init \-\-encryption=$BORG_ENCRYPTION $REPO_URI" .
.TP
.B backup
Create one archive from
.BR BACKUP_PATH ,
honouring
.B BACKUP_EXCLUDE
and
.BR BORG_OPT .
Runs
.BR "borg create" .
The command line is built as an array, so exclusion patterns containing spaces
are handled correctly;
.B BORG_OPT
and
.B BACKUP_PATH
are deliberately word-split. If
.B ARCHIVE_NAME
is empty, an archive named
.BI archive\- YYYYmmdd \- HHMMSS
is generated. Requires
.BR BACKUP_PATH .
.TP
.B list
List all archives in the repository, or, when
.B \-\-archive
is given, the files contained in that archive. The cheapest way to verify
credentials and connectivity.
.TP
.B mount
Mount the repository, or a single archive when
.B \-\-archive
is given, at
.B MOUNT_PATH
via FUSE. The mount point is created if missing. On success the unmount
command is printed;
.B borg mount
daemonises, so the mount outlives the process and must be released with
.BR "borg umount" .
Requires FUSE support in Borg.
.TP
.B check
Without
.BR \-\-archive ,
runs
.BR "borg check \-\-repository\-only" :
a fast structural check that does not read archive data, cheap enough to
schedule weekly. With
.BR \-\-archive ,
runs a full consistency check of that archive.
.B \-\-repair
is never passed.
.TP
.B prune
Delete archives that no retention rule requires. Runs
.B "borg prune \-\-list"
with one
.BI \-\-keep\- period = N
option per configured rule, so the log shows exactly which archives were kept
and pruned. A rule counts as configured when its value is non-empty and not
.BR 0 .
If no rule is configured the operation
.B refuses to run
and reports a failure, rather than pruning everything. Pruning does not release
disk space; run
.B compact
afterwards.
.TP
.B compact
Rewrite repository segments to physically reclaim the space freed by
.B prune
and
.BR delete .
Requires Borg 1.2 or newer.
.TP
.B info
Display repository statistics, or statistics for a single archive when
.B \-\-archive
is given.
.TP
.B delete
Delete the archive named by
.BR \-\-archive ,
which is
.BR mandatory :
without it the operation fails immediately, before contacting the repository.
This is a deliberate guard, since a bare
.B borg delete
would target the entire repository. Destructive and not undoable; space is
reclaimed only after
.BR compact .
.TP
.B extract
Restore the contents of the archive named by
.BR \-\-archive ,
which is
.BR mandatory .
The destination is
.B \-\-path
(or
.BR RESTORE_PATH ),
falling back to the current working directory when neither is set. The
directory is created if missing, and extraction runs in a subshell that changes
into it, leaving the caller's working directory untouched. Paths are restored
relative to that directory, so always extract into a scratch directory and
inspect the result before moving files into place.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " FILE"
.TQ
.BI \-c " DIR" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " DIR"
Configuration file to source. Default:
.I /etc/tngbackup.conf
or the value of
.BR TNGB_CONFIG .
If the argument is a
.BR directory ,
.B batch mode
is used: every
.I *.conf
file directly inside it is processed in turn, in alphabetical order. See
.B "BATCH MODE"
below.
.TP
.BI \-r " URI" "\fR, \fP" \-\-repo " URI"
Repository URI, setting
.BR REPO_URI .
Either a local absolute path or an
.B ssh://
URI. Re-applied after the configuration file is sourced, so it overrides the
file. Ignored in batch mode.
.TP
.BI \-p " PASSPHRASE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-passphrase " PASSPHRASE"
Repository passphrase, setting
.BR REPO_PASSPHRASE .
Re-applied after the configuration file, so it overrides the file. Ignored in
batch mode. Avoid this option on multi-user systems: process command lines are
visible to every user through
.BR ps (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-archive " NAME"
Archive name, setting
.BR ARCHIVE_NAME .
Optional for
.BR backup ,
.BR list ,
.BR check ,
.B info
and
.BR mount ;
.B required
by
.B delete
and
.BR extract .
.TP
.BI \-\-path " PATH"
Context-dependent. With
.B backup
it sets
.BR BACKUP_PATH ;
with every other operation, including
.BR extract ,
it sets
.BR RESTORE_PATH .
Note that with
.B extract
this is the restore
.IR destination ,
not a selector for what to restore.
.TP
.BI \-\-mountpoint " PATH"
Mount directory for
.BR mount ,
setting
.BR MOUNT_PATH .
Default
.IR /mnt/borg .
Created automatically if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-l " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-log " FILE"
Human-readable log file. The file is created immediately and its mode set to
0600; if the path is not writable the run aborts before doing any work. Borg's
combined output is also written here. Once configured, a later failure to write
to the log is tolerated and never aborts an operation. When no log file is
given, Borg output goes to standard output unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-log " FILE"
Structured audit log file. Created immediately with mode 0600. One record is
appended per completed operation; see
.B FILES
below for the format.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the built-in help text and exit with status 0.
.PP
Options are parsed with GNU
.BR getopt (1);
both
.BI \-\-config= FILE
and
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
are accepted. An unrecognised option prints the help text and exits with
status 1.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is a Bash fragment. Quote every value; use single
quotes for passphrases so that
.BR $ ,
.B `
and
.B \e
are taken literally.
.SS Core
.TP
.B TNGB_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file or directory. Default
.IR /etc/tngbackup.conf .
.TP
.B DEBUG
.RB ( y / n ,
default
.BR n )
Print diagnostic output, including the exact Borg command line and the
retention policy in force.
.TP
.B DRYRUN
.RB ( y / n ,
default
.BR n )
Log each Borg command as
.RB \(dq [DRY\-RUN]\ borg\ ... \(dq
and execute nothing. Directory creation for
.B mount
and
.B extract
is skipped as well. Independent of
.BR DEBUG ;
there is no command line flag. Configuration validation still applies, and the
operation always reports success because nothing ran.
.TP
.B SHOWTEXT
.RB ( y / n ,
default
.BR y )
Print timestamped informational messages to the console.
.TP
.B BACKUP
.RB ( y / n ,
default
.BR y )
Compatibility flag carried over from version 1. The version 2 dispatcher
selects work by operation name.
.SS Repository
.TP
.B REPO_URI
Required. Local absolute path or
.BI ssh:// user@host:port/path
URI. Exported to Borg as
.BR BORG_REPO .
An
.B ssh://
URI also triggers automatic
.B BORG_RSH
configuration.
.TP
.B REPO_PASSPHRASE
Required. Exported to Borg as
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE .
Validation rejects an empty value even for an unencrypted repository.
.TP
.B BORG_ENCRYPTION
Encryption mode for
.BR init .
Default
.BR repokey\-blake2 .
.SS Backup
.TP
.B BACKUP_PATH
.B Space
-separated list of paths to archive. Required for
.BR backup .
Paths containing spaces are not supported.
.TP
.B BACKUP_EXCLUDE
.B Semicolon
-separated list of exclusion patterns; note the different separator from
.BR BACKUP_PATH .
Each non-empty item becomes one
.B \-\-exclude
argument, and patterns containing spaces are handled correctly. Default empty.
.TP
.B ARCHIVE_NAME
Archive name. If empty,
.BI archive\- YYYYmmdd \- HHMMSS
is generated.
.TP
.B RESTORE_PATH
Destination directory for
.BR extract .
Falls back to the current working directory when unset.
.TP
.B MOUNT_PATH
Mount point for
.BR mount .
Default
.IR /mnt/borg .
.SS Borg and SSH
.TP
.B BORG_OPT
Extra options passed to
.B "borg create"
only. Word-split, so no single argument may contain spaces.
.TP
.B SSH_OPT
SSH client options used to build
.B BORG_RSH
for
.B ssh://
repositories. Default:
.BR "\-o BatchMode=yes \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept\-new \-o ConnectTimeout=5" .
Overriding it replaces the defaults wholesale, so repeat the options you still
want.
.TP
.B SSH_PORT
SSH port for remote repositories. Default 22.
.PP
Setting
.B BORG_RSH
by hand is not necessary. Export
.B BORG_REMOTE_PATH
from the configuration file if
.B borg
is not on the server's non-interactive
.BR PATH .
.SS Retention
.TP
.BR KEEP_LAST ", " KEEP_HOURLY ", " KEEP_DAILY ", " KEEP_WEEKLY ", " KEEP_MONTHLY ", " KEEP_YEARLY
Integer retention rules used by
.BR prune ,
mapping to Borg's
.BI \-\-keep\- period
options. Each rule keeps the newest archive in each time bucket; the archives
kept are the union of all rules, so the rules overlap rather than add up. A
value that is empty or
.B 0
disables that rule.
.PP
Unlike most settings these are
.B not
empty by default:
.BR KEEP_LAST=10 ,
.BR KEEP_DAILY=7 ,
.B KEEP_WEEKLY=4
and
.BR KEEP_MONTHLY=12 ,
with
.B KEEP_HOURLY
and
.B KEEP_YEARLY
unset. A configuration file that never mentions retention therefore still has
a real policy, and running
.B prune
against it will delete archives outside that policy. Set the variables
explicitly, and confirm the effective policy with
.B "DEBUG=y DRYRUN=y tngbackup prune"
before the first prune on any repository. Setting all six to
.B 0
does not prune everything: it makes
.B prune
refuse to run.
.SS Logging
.TP
.B LOG_FILE
Human-readable log file path. Empty by default, in which case Borg output goes
to standard output.
.TP
.B AUDIT_LOG_FILE
Audit log file path. If empty, auditing is disabled silently.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH "BATCH MODE"
When
.B \-\-config
names a directory, every
.I *.conf
file directly inside it is processed sequentially, in alphabetical order. For
each file the script resets
.BR REPO_URI ,
.BR REPO_PASSPHRASE ,
.BR BACKUP_PATH ,
.B BACKUP_EXCLUDE
and
.B ARCHIVE_NAME
to empty, sources the file, validates the result and runs the requested
operation through the same dispatcher used by single-config runs. All ten
operations are supported, although
.BR backup ,
.B check
and
.B prune
are the ones that make sense unattended.
.PP
A file that fails to source, fails validation, or whose operation fails is
logged and counted; the batch continues. When the batch finishes,
.B tngbackup
.B exits non-zero if any configuration failed,
so a zero exit status does mean every job at least completed. An operation that
finished with a Borg warning is
.B not
counted as a batch failure, for the same reason it does not fail a single-config
run: it produced its result. Such runs are still distinguishable in the audit
log by their
.B WARNING
status.
.PP
Variables other than the five listed above are
.B not
reset between files. In particular
.BR BORG_OPT ,
all six
.BI KEEP_ *
rules,
.BR RESTORE_PATH ,
.BR MOUNT_PATH ,
.B SSH_OPT
and
.B SSH_PORT
carry over from the previously processed configuration. Set every variable you
care about explicitly in every batch configuration file, including empty ones;
an inherited retention rule is a silent data-retention bug.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH TESTS
.TP
.I tests/dispatch\-test.sh
26 checks that run anywhere, with
.B no Borg installation required:
a stub
.B borg
is placed first on
.B PATH
and records the command line it receives. Covers operation dispatch, argument
construction, retention options, command-line precedence, dry-run, the
success/warning/failure exit-code mapping, failure propagation, audit-log
format, absence of passphrase leakage into the logs, and batch mode. Exits
.B 0
when all checks pass,
.B 1
otherwise.
.TP
.I tests/integration\-test.sh
End-to-end test against a real Borg installation. Creates a throwaway
repository in a temporary directory, exercises every operation against it,
verifies the audit log and removes everything. Set
.B TNGB_TEST_MOUNT=y
to exercise
.B mount
as well, which additionally requires FUSE. Exits
.B 0
when all tests pass,
.B 1
on failure, and
.B 2
when prerequisites are missing, so a CI job can treat
.B 2
as skipped.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tngbackup.conf
Default configuration file. Installed with mode 0600 by
.IR install.sh ,
which never overwrites an existing one.
.TP
.I /etc/tngbackup/
Conventional batch configuration directory. Should be mode 0700, containing
files of mode 0600.
.TP
.I /var/log/tngbackup.log
Conventional log file, created mode 0600 by
.IR install.sh .
Records have the form
.RS
.PP
.RI [ YYYY\-MM\-DD
.IR HH:MM:SS ]
.RI [ LEVEL ]
.I message
.RE
.TP
.I /var/log/tngbackup-audit.log
Conventional audit log, created mode 0600 by
.IR install.sh .
One pipe-delimited record per completed operation:
.RS
.PP
.I TIMESTAMP_UTC_ISO8601
|
.I operation
|
.I status
|
.I details
|
.I duration_seconds
.PP
for example:
.PP
.nf
2026\-09\-05T03:04:47Z | backup | SUCCESS | archive\-20260905\-030001 | 286
2026\-09\-05T04:01:38Z | check | SUCCESS | repository | 97
2026\-09\-05T04:30:12Z | prune | SUCCESS | \-\-keep\-last=10 \-\-keep\-daily=7 | 12
2026\-09\-05T03:09:22Z | backup | WARNING | archive\-20260905\-030512 | 250
2026\-09\-05T03:14:30Z | backup | FAILED | archive\-20260905\-030955 | 275
.fi
.PP
.I status
is
.BR SUCCESS ,
.B WARNING
or
.BR FAILED .
.B WARNING
records a Borg exit code of 1: the operation completed - the archive on that
line exists and can be restored from - but something was noteworthy, typically
a file that could not be read. A monitoring rule that greps for
.B FAILED
therefore ignores warnings correctly;
.\" keep both statuses in mind when alerting on missing backups
match
.B SUCCESS
and
.B WARNING
together when checking that a backup happened at all.
.PP
.I details
carries the archive name, the repository URI,
.BR all\-archives ,
.BR repository ,
the retention options applied by
.BR prune ,
or
.IR "ARCHIVE \-> TARGET"
for
.B mount
and
.BR extract ;
.I duration_seconds
counts whole seconds since the script started. In batch mode each
configuration contributes its own record.
.RE
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tngbackup
Default installation path of the script.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tngbackup.service
.TQ
.I /etc/systemd/system/tngbackup.timer
Optional systemd units for scheduled operation, installed by
.IR install.sh .
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B tngbackup
exits with the exit code of the operation it performed.
.TP
.B 0
Success,
.B or completed with warnings.
Also returned by
.B \-\-help
and by choosing
.B 0
in the interactive menu.
.TP
.B 1
Failure detected by the script itself, before or instead of running Borg:
invalid option, configuration file not found or unreadable, failed
validation (missing
.BR REPO_URI ,
.B REPO_PASSPHRASE
or
.BR BACKUP_PATH ,
or
.B borg
not found in
.BR PATH ),
unknown operation, a missing
.B \-\-archive
for
.B delete
or
.BR extract ,
no retention policy configured for
.BR prune ,
or an unwritable mountpoint or restore directory. In batch mode, at least one
configuration failed.
.TP
.B 2 and above
Propagated from Borg itself
.RB ( 2
is Borg's own \(lqerror\(rq), or from the shell under
.BR "set \-euo pipefail" :
for example
.B 126
or
.B 127
for a command that could not be executed,
.B 130
for interruption by
.BR SIGINT ,
.B 143
for
.BR SIGTERM .
.PP
Borg's convention is
.B 0
success,
.B 1
warning,
.B 2
error, and it is mapped deliberately:
.RS
.TP
.B 0
Recorded
.BR SUCCESS ,
exits
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B 1
Recorded
.BR WARNING ,
logged at
.B WARN
level, and
.B exits 0.
The operation completed and produced its result; something was merely
noteworthy, most often a file that could not be read or that vanished
mid-backup. Such a backup is a usable backup, so it does not fail the run, mark
a systemd unit failed, or generate cron mail.
.TP
.B "2 or higher"
Recorded
.BR FAILED ,
and the exit code passes through unchanged.
.RE
.PP
Note the asymmetry: an exit code of
.B 1
from
.B tngbackup
never means \(lqBorg warned\(rq - it means the script rejected the request
before Borg ran. Borg's own warnings never reach the caller as a non-zero
status.
.PP
Warnings likewise do not count as batch failures, so a batch run that exits
.B 0
means every configuration at least completed.
.\" ==========================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Install, then create a repository and take a first backup:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo ./install.sh
sudo \(dq${EDITOR:\-vi}\(dq /etc/tngbackup.conf
tngbackup init   \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
tngbackup backup \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
.RE
.fi
.PP
Preview what a backup would do, without touching the repository:
.PP
.nf
.RS
DEBUG=y DRYRUN=y tngbackup backup \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
.RE
.fi
.PP
Take a backup with an explicit archive name and full logging:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup backup \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf \e
    \-\-archive pre\-upgrade\-2026\-09\-05 \e
    \-\-log /var/log/tngbackup.log \e
    \-\-audit\-log /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
List all archives, then the contents of one of them:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup list \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
tngbackup list \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf \-\-archive archive\-20260905\-030001
.RE
.fi
.PP
Ad-hoc listing of a repository with no configuration file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup list \-\-repo /mnt/backup/borg\-repo \-\-passphrase 'secret'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Restore an archive into a scratch directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup extract \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf \e
    \-\-archive archive\-20260905\-030001 \-\-path /tmp/restore
.RE
.fi
.PP
Mount an archive for browsing, then unmount it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup mount \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf \e
    \-\-archive archive\-20260905\-030001 \-\-mountpoint /mnt/borg
borg umount /mnt/borg
.RE
.fi
.PP
Confirm the retention policy, apply it, and reclaim the freed space:
.PP
.nf
.RS
DEBUG=y DRYRUN=y tngbackup prune \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
tngbackup prune   \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
tngbackup compact \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf
.RE
.fi
.PP
Back up every job in a batch configuration directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tngbackup backup \-\-config /etc/tngbackup/ \e
    \-\-log /var/log/tngbackup.log \e
    \-\-audit\-log /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
A nightly
.BR crontab (5)
entry:
.PP
.nf
.RS
0 3 * * * /usr/local/bin/tngbackup backup \-\-config /etc/tngbackup.conf \e
    \-\-log /var/log/tngbackup.log \-\-audit\-log /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check the audit log for failures recorded today, then for warnings separately:
.PP
.nf
.RS
today=$(date \-u +%Y\-%m\-%d)
grep \(dq^$today\(dq /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log | grep '| FAILED |'
grep \(dq^$today\(dq /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log | grep '| WARNING |'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Verify that a backup happened at all today, counting a warning as a backup:
.PP
.nf
.RS
grep \-qE \(dq^$(date \-u +%Y\-%m\-%d).*\e| backup \e| (SUCCESS|WARNING) \e|\(dq \e
    /var/log/tngbackup\-audit.log || echo \(dqNO BACKUP TODAY\(dq
.RE
.fi
